<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<!--
  Entitlements for the macOS SCREENSHOT / LAUNCH-SMOKE build. Never shipped:
  the Mac App Store artifact uses BirdoVPN-macOS.entitlements.

  EMPTY, and that is the conclusion of a measured dead end rather than an
  oversight. Read this before adding a key back.

  An ad-hoc signature carries no team, so launchd cannot validate ANY
  team-prefixed entitlement and refuses to spawn the binary outright:

      Could not launch "BirdoVPN". Runningboard has returned error 5.
      Launchd job spawn failed.

  Measured, one key at a time:

      (no entitlements)                    app LAUNCHES, sign-in fails
      application-identifier + keychain    app will NOT launch
      keychain-access-groups alone         app will NOT launch

  And sign-in cannot work without one: KeychainService writes with
  kSecAttrAccessGroup and kSecUseDataProtectionKeychain, and the
  data-protection keychain refuses an app that has no access group —
  "Could not securely store your session. Please try again."

  So the build can LAUNCH or it can reach the KEYCHAIN, never both. The way
  out is not another entitlement; it is a real signature, and that is blocked
  further up:

      keychain -> team entitlement -> real signature -> provisioning profile
      -> XCUITest needs get-task-allow=true -> DEVELOPMENT profile
      -> macOS development profiles are bound to REGISTERED DEVICES
      -> an ephemeral CI runner is not one

  (A distribution profile is not a way around it: those set
  get-task-allow=false, so the test runner cannot attach at all.)

  What the job is therefore good for, and it is not nothing: it launches the
  real macOS app in a real GUI session and drives its UI. It is the only
  automated check that the Mac build RUNS rather than merely compiles and
  signs, and it caught nothing less than the app rendering correctly on macOS
  for the first time. Treat it as a launch smoke test.

  Store screenshots need a Mac that is a registered device — where a
  development profile can be issued, and where the one-time Accessibility
  ("automation mode") permission has been granted at the console.
-->
<dict/>
</plist>
